    {
      "id": "piano.s7.l1",
      "title": "Hands together: first steps",
      "summary": "Play both hands at once in parallel and contrary motion.",
      "minutes": 11,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "How to practice hands together",
          "body": "Playing with both hands at once is a new coordination skill, separate from knowing each part.\n\n1. Learn each hand alone first until it is easy.\n2. Put them together **much slower** than either hand alone. Wait mode is fine.\n3. Watch the moments where both hands play at the same time; those are the checkpoints.\n4. Speed up only after several clean runs.\n\n**Parallel motion:** both hands move in the same direction. **Contrary motion:** the hands move in opposite directions (mirror image)."
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Mirror fingering",
          "body": "Contrary motion from C position is easier than it looks: put both thumbs near the middle, **LH 1 on G3** and **RH 1 on C4**. Moving outward, both hands use the **same finger numbers** at the same time: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.\n\nIn parallel motion (both hands in C position, LH on C3, RH on C4) the finger numbers differ: when the RH plays 1, the LH plays 5.",
          "diagram": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "notes": [
              "C3",
              "D3",
              "E3",
              "F3",
              "G3",
              "C4",
