    {
      "id": "piano.s7.l2",
      "title": "Block-chord accompaniment",
      "summary": "Hold a left-hand chord under a right-hand melody.",
      "minutes": 12,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Melody and accompaniment",
          "body": "Most piano music splits into a **melody** (the tune, often in the right hand) and an **accompaniment** (the harmony and rhythm supporting it, often in the left hand).\n\nThe simplest accompaniment is **block chords**: the LH plays all the chord's notes together and holds them. Use the closest-position LH chords from Stage 5: **C = C3 E3 G3**, **G/B = B2 D3 G3**.\n\nThe chord changes when the melody's harmony changes, usually at the start of a measure. When one hand holds while the other moves, keep the held keys down; do not let them bounce up."
        },
