      "id": "piano.s7.l3",
      "title": "Broken chords and Alberti bass",
      "summary": "Spread chord notes out in time for a flowing accompaniment.",
      "minutes": 13,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Broken chords",
          "body": "A **broken chord** plays the chord's notes one at a time instead of together. An **arpeggio** is a broken chord played in order up or down (C E G C).\n\nThe **Alberti bass** is a broken-chord pattern named after an 18th-century composer: **lowest, highest, middle, highest**. For C (C3 E3 G3): **C3 G3 E3 G3**, usually in eighth notes. Mozart and Haydn used it constantly.\n\n- C: C3 G3 E3 G3\n- F/C: C3 A3 F3 A3\n- G/B: B2 G3 D3 G3\n\nLH fingering: **5 1 3 1**. The thumb repeats; keep it light.",
          "diagram": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "notes": [
